  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia supported the three (3) provincial governments of Catanduanes, Laguna, and Maguindanao to implement the &apos;Minimum Initial Service Package (MISP) for Sexual and Reproductive Health (SRH) in Emergencies&apos; &#x2013; a priority set of life-saving SRH activities to be implemented at the onset of every humanitarian crisis. The provincial governments identified a total of approximately one billion pesos in their budget for the next three years to fund their multi-year costed implementation plans for MISP-SRH to ensure women and girls have access to comprehensive SRH and gender-based violence information and services.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency assistance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia, through its humanitarian partners, provided around AUD6 million worth of assistance to 135,561 persons affected by Typhoon Rai across six (6) provinces (Negros Occidental, Bohol, Cebu, Southern Leyte, Dinagat Islands, and Surigao del Norte), enabling affected communities to meet basic needs and restore their livelihoods. Support included food/non-food items, cash, Sexual and Reproductive Health and Gender Based Violence protection supplies and services, shelter repair and hygiene kits, and livelihood assistance. The support was timely as most partners delivered within a reasonable timeframe (7-10 days) or adjusted their strategy to ensure assistance reached the beneficiaries sooner.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gender equality</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2022, CARE documented three (3) cases of gender-based violence (one of which involved a 14-year-old) in Negros Occidental. The three (3) cases were referred to the Women and Children Protection Desk (WCPD) and to the Municipal Social Welfare and Development Office (MSWDO) which provided them access to financial assistance and guided them through the process of filing a case against the abuser. The Australia-supported project shouldered mobility costs and facilitated access of survivors and their family members to counselling sessions.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Philippine Red Cross supported the Philippine Government&#x2019;s COVID-19 response efforts by establishing a molecular laboratory; procured a negative pressure ambulance (Skippy) to transport COVID patients; and provided 1 million items of personal protective equipment to medical frontliners. From 2020-2022, the molecular laboratory processed a total of 2,089,962 tests; while Skippy transported 192 patients (not the 600 previously reported). The previous Skippy numbers reflected &#x2018;all&#x2019; ambulance runs not just COVID-19 patients.   Due to the decreasing COVID cases in 2022, the molecular laboratory processed 197,499 samples; while Skippy transported 12 patients.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
